1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,786
Trial worked because Priya read everything.

2
00:00:02,786 --> 00:00:06,130
Nobody reads everything forever, and nobody should have to.

3
00:00:06,130 --> 00:00:10,662
Once an agent earns its way onto the Review rung, checking becomes sampling, and

4
00:00:10,662 --> 00:00:13,666
sampling only works if the sample is chosen honestly.

5
00:00:13,666 --> 00:00:18,029
Honestly is the hard word in that sentence, so ask it plainly: which run gets

6
00:00:18,029 --> 00:00:19,445
checked, and who decides?

7
00:00:19,445 --> 00:00:24,205
Left to instinct, you will sample the outputs that were already on your screen, skip

8
00:00:24,205 --> 00:00:28,681
the weeks you were busy, and gravitate to runs that look interesting, which are

9
00:00:28,681 --> 00:00:31,061
precisely the ones drift does not live in.

10
00:00:31,061 --> 00:00:35,820
So take the choosing away from yourself: Section 1.2's die roll, or anything equally

11
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:40,240
blind, picks the run, and a ten minute calendar slot on Friday picks the time.

12
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:44,490
A spot check that depends on remembering and choosing is two failure points

13
00:00:44,490 --> 00:00:45,906
pretending to be a habit.

14
00:00:45,906 --> 00:00:47,719
Booked and blind, it is neither.

15
00:00:48,269 --> 00:00:52,752
Read the middle row again: a weekly agent like Agent 3 stays fully checked for

16
00:00:52,752 --> 00:00:55,626
twelve straight weeks before sampling even begins.

17
00:00:55,626 --> 00:01:00,166
That is not overcaution, it is roughly the length of trial season for a renewal

18
00:01:00,166 --> 00:01:04,822
pipeline, long enough to see a sheet actually get edited by someone else at least

19
00:01:04,822 --> 00:01:05,109
once.

20
00:01:05,109 --> 00:01:08,730
The rates differ because evidence accrues per run, not per day.

21
00:01:08,730 --> 00:01:13,442
A daily agent hands you thirty verdicts a month, so a random one each week keeps a

22
00:01:13,442 --> 00:01:14,477
real finger on it.

23
00:01:14,477 --> 00:01:19,305
A weekly agent produces four, which is why it stays fully read for so long: sampling

24
00:01:19,305 --> 00:01:21,834
four outputs would be a ritual, not a check.

25
00:01:21,834 --> 00:01:26,144
And the third row's trailing clause, after any prompt change, is the one to

26
00:01:26,144 --> 00:01:26,719
underline.

27
00:01:26,719 --> 00:01:31,489
Any edit to the briefing resets part of the trust, because the agent you checked is

28
00:01:31,489 --> 00:01:33,903
no longer quite the agent that is running.

29
00:01:33,903 --> 00:01:38,156
The you-in-three-months from Section 6.4, adding one small thing on a busy

30
00:01:38,156 --> 00:01:40,972
afternoon, is exactly who that clause exists for.

31
00:01:41,522 --> 00:01:44,702
Question five is the one built directly from Section 7.3.

32
00:01:44,702 --> 00:01:48,831
A moved row is exactly the kind of thing "drift" is designed to catch on a

33
00:01:48,831 --> 00:01:52,012
Review-rung agent that nobody is reading in full anymore.

34
00:01:52,012 --> 00:01:56,698
Run the five once, out loud, against a sampled chaser draft, and feel how mechanical

35
00:01:56,698 --> 00:01:57,033
it is.

36
00:01:57,033 --> 00:02:01,050
Facts: the renewal date in the draft, found in the sheet, cell for cell.

37
00:02:01,050 --> 00:02:04,231
Scope: the draft concerns a renewal client, nothing else.

38
00:02:04,231 --> 00:02:08,471
Ceiling: read it once looking only for money and pressure, and find neither.

39
00:02:08,471 --> 00:02:12,265
Tone: read it aloud as if signing it, the Section 6 test, unchanged.

40
00:02:12,265 --> 00:02:16,562
Drift: put it next to a draft from a month ago and ask what is different, and

41
00:02:16,562 --> 00:02:17,956
whether you can name why.

42
00:02:17,956 --> 00:02:22,532
Ten minutes, five verdicts, and the useful discipline is writing Pass next to each

43
00:02:22,532 --> 00:02:26,940
question rather than nodding: a nod cannot be audited in March, and a filled-in

44
00:02:26,940 --> 00:02:27,553
rubric can.

45
00:02:28,103 --> 00:02:31,870
The protocol in that box is the difference between fixing and hoping.

46
00:02:31,870 --> 00:02:36,400
A quiet prompt patch is an untested change to a system you have just learned not to

47
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:40,931
fully trust, made in response to a failure you have not yet explained: two unknowns

48
00:02:40,931 --> 00:02:41,531
multiplied.

49
00:02:41,531 --> 00:02:46,062
Moving the failure back to Trial costs you a week of closer reading, and re-testing

50
00:02:46,062 --> 00:02:50,483
against the exact case that failed is what proves the fix fixed something, rather

51
00:02:50,483 --> 00:02:53,158
than the failure simply not recurring on its own.

52
00:02:53,158 --> 00:02:54,795
Machines get regression tests.

53
00:02:54,795 --> 00:02:59,380
Your agents deserve the plain-English version: the thing that broke it once gets run

54
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:01,564
again, on purpose, before trust resumes.

55
00:03:01,564 --> 00:03:06,094
Fill in C07 against one of your own agents this week, even if you have not moved it

56
00:03:06,094 --> 00:03:06,858
to Review yet.

57
00:03:06,858 --> 00:03:11,444
Next lecture names the failure modes worth watching for before you meet them for the

58
00:03:11,444 --> 00:03:12,044
first time.
